{"kind":"report","text":"Closing d6504b10. Public host identity resolution now decides authority by verified record position, and the fix is merged on main at dea7c0a8479673fe25d98e3ac7944084377fa80d.\n\nI did not implement this. I promised it, left the worktree clean at main while it blocked chess item 4 and item 7, and you asked three times before I acted. What I finally did was file scoped child 3d8ad413 to codex adopting the design codex had already worked out, and codex implemented it. That is the honest account of this commitment: the useful decision was to stop holding it.\n\nWHAT LANDED, verified at origin/main rather than recalled. host/identity/resolve.go now models a canonical instant of {position, timestamp}. An anchor cannot reach backward -- at.position \u003c anchor.from.position is refused -- and a revocation applies from its own recorded position, at.position \u003e= anchor.revoked.position. Delegation and the recursive parent walk decide on the same quantity. An unknown or changed record identifier resolves empty rather than falling through to something permissive. Signed time survives only where it means something externally, in NotAfter expiry.\n\nWHY THAT SATISFIES THE REQUEST. The defect you named was that a later anchor or revocation could retroactively change how an earlier application act resolved, because two records in the same second were judged by a quantity that could tie. Position cannot tie, and it is an immutable fact of the verified log rather than a claim a writer makes about the clock. That is the same rule we already accepted for irreversible operations: gate on what the log records, not on what a record asserts.\n\nThe review was independent and the head was approved, ratified and merged; live succession sits at host artifact 5774cd01 and docs artifact dbcb354b. Chess item 4 can publish and item 7 can rebase.\n\nThe child commitment closed on its own merge receipt. This report closes the parent.","body":{"commit":"dea7c0a8479673fe25d98e3ac7944084377fa80d"}}